        <div id="why-cords" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why New Dryers Come Without a Cord
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy an electric dryer in the United States and there is a good chance it comes without a power cord. That surprises people, but it is deliberate. American homes have two different 30-amp dryer outlets in common use, the older three-prong NEMA 10-30 and the newer four-prong NEMA 14-30, and the dryer manufacturer cannot know which one you have. So the dryer ships with a terminal block and a bonding strap, and you or the installer add the cord that matches your wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes choosing the best dryer cord a simple job, and a surprisingly important one. A cord that matches your outlet, carries a listing mark, reaches without strain, and is installed with the bonding strap set the way the dryer manual says is a safe, permanent connection. A cord that does not match, or an adapter bolted on to force a mismatch, is how dryer installs go wrong. The cord is cheap; getting it right is what matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that the cords themselves are commodities. A four-wire 30-amp cord from one listed brand is electrically the same as another, and the real choices are type and length. The products below were chosen because they are widely available under clear names, come in both three- and four-wire versions, and are what you will find at home centers and appliance stores.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is about electric dryers. Gas dryers run on a standard 120-volt cord and plug into an ordinary household outlet for the drum motor, controls and igniter, so they never need a 30-amp dryer cord. If you are in a laundry room thinking about electrical upgrades more broadly, our pillar guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole-house surge protectors</a> covers protection for the dryer, washer and every other appliance on the panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outlets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Three-Prong vs Four-Prong Dryer Outlets
          </h2>

          <p>
            A three prong dryer outlet is a NEMA 10-30 receptacle. It has two angled slots for the two hot conductors and one L-shaped slot for the neutral, and no separate ground. A four-prong outlet is a NEMA 14-30: two straight vertical slots for the hots, an L-shaped neutral, and a round or U-shaped ground. Both are 30 amp dryer outlet designs on a 120/240-volt circuit, and both are fed by a double-pole 30-amp breaker; the difference is whether the ground has its own wire.
          </p>

          <p>
            That difference matters because of how the dryer's metal cabinet is grounded. In a four-wire setup the cabinet is connected to a dedicated equipment ground, and the neutral carries only the dryer's 120-volt current for the timer, lights and motor. In a three-wire setup, the cabinet is bonded to the neutral, so the neutral does double duty. If that neutral ever loosens or breaks, the dryer cabinet can become energized, which is the reason the code moved away from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 1996 edition of the National Electrical Code began requiring four-wire circuits for new dryer and range installations. States and cities adopt code editions on their own schedules, so the change reached different areas at different times, but most homes wired from the late 1990s on have four-prong outlets. Existing three-wire circuits were allowed to remain in service, which is why millions of older homes still have the three-prong type and why three-wire cords are still sold.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no prize for guessing which one you have. Look at the outlet, count the slots, and compare the shapes to the descriptions above. If the outlet looks damaged, scorched or loose, stop there and call an electrician, because no new cord fixes a failing receptacle. If you need to turn off the circuit to inspect it, a <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder</a> will confirm which double-pole breaker feeds the laundry before you open anything.
          </p>

