        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best dryer cord?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best dryer cord is a listed 30-amp cord whose plug matches your outlet and whose length reaches without strain. For four-prong NEMA 14-30 outlets, a four-wire cord such as the Certified Appliance Accessories four-wire or Whirlpool PT220 is the right choice. For existing three-prong NEMA 10-30 outlets, a three-wire cord such as the Certified Appliance Accessories three-wire is correct. Brand matters far less than type and length.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a 4 prong to 3 prong dryer adapter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not recommended. Adapters between three- and four-prong dryer plugs leave the dryer's grounding mismatched with the wiring and add extra connections on a high-current circuit. Dryer manufacturers' instructions call for a cord that matches the outlet, with the bonding strap set for that cord type. A new cord costs about the same as an adapter and is the safe, accepted fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I convert my dryer from 3 prong to 4 prong?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the wall outlet is already four-prong, replace the dryer's three-wire cord with a four-wire 14-30 cord and remove or relocate the bonding strap as the dryer manual describes. If the wall outlet is three-prong and you want four-prong, that requires adding a ground conductor or a new circuit, which is a job for a licensed electrician, usually with a permit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are three prong dryer outlets still legal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Existing three-prong dryer outlets are generally allowed to remain in service under the National Electrical Code, which began requiring four-wire circuits for new installations in its 1996 edition. New circuits must be four-wire. Manufacturers also say three-wire connections are not permitted in manufactured homes or where local codes prohibit them, so check your situation if you are unsure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size breaker and wire does a 30 amp dryer outlet need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard electric dryer circuit uses a 30-amp double-pole breaker and copper conductors typically sized at 10 AWG, feeding either a NEMA 10-30 or NEMA 14-30 receptacle. Always check your dryer's nameplate and installation instructions. Never install a larger breaker to stop nuisance tripping; a tripping breaker is a symptom for an electrician to diagnose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Smart Choice dryer cord any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Smart Choice is an appliance accessory line associated with Frigidaire and sold through many retailers, and its three- and four-wire dryer cords are rated for 30-amp dryer circuits like other listed cords. There is nothing special about them electrically compared with other listed brands. Choose the version that matches your outlet and the length you need, and confirm whether a strain relief is included.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a dryer cord myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many homeowners replace a dryer cord themselves, since it attaches to the dryer's terminal block rather than the house wiring. Unplug the dryer first, follow the dryer manual's wiring diagram for your cord type, set the bonding strap correctly and install a strain relief. If anything is unclear, or the outlet looks damaged, have an appliance installer or licensed electrician handle it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

